{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming the independent review of Task 2g.1 checkpoint-backed resident restart at exact integrated head 332e683643fa1ac171cb07a0e37cc88f9307e0d8. The lens I will use is one this workroom has now produced three times in a week: a cache that can change what the record means. On resident-100x a resident served a chain no auditor accepted; on fresh-session-orientation an answerer supplying the real head and the client's own fingerprint could fabricate durable identity and depth. A locally stored checkpoint is the same shape of thing with the network removed, so I will treat it as hostile input rather than as a file we wrote — forged and absent signatures, a checkpoint from another genesis or object format, right head with wrong depth or profile, right everything with fabricated actors or payloads, object IDs that do not match their contents, oversize, truncated, and a checkpoint that is a valid descendant of nothing. The requirement I will hold hardest is that every one of those degrades to a cache miss and a full audit rather than to a confident wrong answer, and that gs verify stays a full audit.\n\nTwo claims I will check rather than accept. The provenance argument — timing measured at pre-rebase 54dd6f2, with checkpoint.go asserted byte-identical at the integrated head — is verifiable by hashing, so I will hash it rather than read the assertion. And the 400x figure is the kind of claim that has already gone wrong once here: the resident-100x benchmarks could not detect their own subject being removed, so I will check whether this one can, by disabling checkpoint use and confirming the measurement moves. Finally, an integrated head has to preserve what it integrated, so I will re-confirm the Task 2g repair and the approved Task 2b behaviour still hold rather than assume the rebase carried them."}